<Page path="concepts/overlays">
<section id="order">
  <h2 id="order"><a href="#order">Merge order</a></h2>
  <p>An app is a list of compose files, merged left to right with the last word winning. multiversectl adds two of its own at the end:</p>
  <div class="code"><pre>1. docker-compose.yml          <span class="c"># the repository's own, unchanged</span>
2. overlays/&lt;app&gt;.yml          <span class="c"># beside the manifest, if present</span>
3. .multiverse-overlay-&lt;app&gt;.yml  <span class="c"># the manifest's inline overlay:</span>
4. .multiverse-routes-&lt;app&gt;.yml   <span class="c"># generated: routing, ports, labels</span></pre></div>
  <p>Everything before the generated files is yours. Nothing rewrites your compose file, and removing multiversectl leaves a repository that still runs with plain <code>docker compose up</code>.</p>
  <p>Compose's merge rules decide the rest: mappings merge key by key, so a later <code>environment:</code> entry replaces just that variable, while sequences are replaced whole, so a later <code>command:</code> or <code>ports:</code> supersedes the earlier one entirely.</p>
</section>

<section id="where">
  <h2 id="where"><a href="#where">Which file to put something in</a></h2>
  <div class="tw"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">The value is</th><th scope="col">Put it</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td data-l="The value is">True of the multiverse everywhere</td><td data-l="Put it">The repository's own compose file.</td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="The value is">True of this environment, for a repository you control</td><td data-l="Put it">A label on the service — see <a href="labels.html">Labels</a>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="The value is">True of this environment, for a repository you do not control</td><td data-l="Put it">The app's inline <code>overlay:</code> in the manifest.</td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="The value is">Several services' worth, or long</td><td data-l="Put it">A file beside the manifest, named in <code>compose:</code> with an <code>@</code> prefix.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>Prefer the label: it keeps the knowledge in the repository that has it, and it is inert when the compose file is run by hand.</p>
</section>

<section id="conventions">
  <h2 id="conventions"><a href="#conventions">Found by convention</a></h2>
  <p>With no <code>compose:</code> list, whichever of these exist are used, in order:</p>
  <ul class="t">
    <li><code>docker-compose.yml</code> in the repository's checkout.</li>
    <li><code>overlays/&lt;app&gt;.yml</code> beside the manifest — or <code>&lt;manifest-name&gt;/&lt;app&gt;.yml</code>, for a directory of several manifests.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>So an app that follows the convention needs no <code>compose:</code> line, and a repository no <code>apps:</code> entry names gets an app of its own name on the same terms. When you do name files, a plain path is relative to the repository's checkout and one starting with <code>@</code> to the manifest's directory:</p>
  <div class="code"><pre><span class="k">apps</span>:
  - <span class="k">name</span>: api
    <span class="k">repo</span>: api
    <span class="k">compose</span>: [docker-compose.yml, docker-compose.dev.yml, <span class="s">"@acme/api.yml"</span>]</pre></div>
</section>

<section id="inline">
  <h2 id="inline"><a href="#inline">The inline overlay</a></h2>
  <p><code>overlay:</code> is a compose fragment written straight into the manifest, kept verbatim and merged third. It is compose syntax, so it can use any of the <a href="../reference/variables.html">variables a universe passes</a>:</p>
  <div class="code"><pre><span class="k">apps</span>:
  - <span class="k">name</span>: web
    <span class="k">repo</span>: web
    <span class="k">profiles</span>: [dev]
    <span class="k">overlay</span>:
      <span class="k">services</span>:
        <span class="k">app-dev</span>:
          <span class="c"># The dev server rejects Host headers it does not know,</span>
          <span class="c"># and this universe's hostname is not in its config.</span>
          <span class="k">command</span>: sh -c <span class="s">"npm start -- --allowed-hosts=$&#123;APP_HOST&#125;"</span></pre></div>
  <p>It is also the whole service definition for a repository with no compose file of its own:</p>
  <div class="code"><pre><span class="k">apps</span>:
  - <span class="k">name</span>: docs
    <span class="k">repo</span>: docs
    <span class="k">overlay</span>:
      <span class="k">services</span>:
        <span class="k">site</span>:
          <span class="k">image</span>: nginx:alpine
          <span class="k">volumes</span>: [<span class="s">"$&#123;DOCS_DIR&#125;:/usr/share/nginx/html:ro"</span>]
          <span class="k">labels</span>:
            <span class="k">dev.multiversectl.host.docs</span>: <span class="s">"80"</span></pre></div>
</section>

<section id="generated">
  <h2 id="generated"><a href="#generated">The generated fragment</a></h2>
  <p>Written before each start, and where the environment-specific mechanics live:</p>
  <ul class="t">
    <li><strong>Proxy labels</strong> for every host rule, naming the app network the proxy reaches the service on.</li>
    <li><strong><code>ports: !reset []</code></strong> on every service not allowed to publish — <code>!reset</code> clears a sequence an earlier file set rather than adding to it.</li>
    <li><strong>An <code>environment:</code> block</strong> for the values a service asked for by label.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Merged last, so its <code>environment:</code> beats both the service's own and any <code>env_file:</code>. That is what lets a per-universe value override a static list in a committed <code>.env</code> — the one thing an overlay in the manifest cannot do.</p>
</section>

<section id="profiles">
  <h2 id="profiles"><a href="#profiles">Profiles and service selection</a></h2>
  <div class="tw"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td data-l="Field"><code>profiles</code></td><td data-l="Meaning">Compose profiles always active for this app, so a <code>dev</code>-profiled service starts and a <code>prod</code>-profiled one does not.</td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Field"><code>services</code></td><td data-l="Meaning">The services to start. Omit it and every service defined for the active profiles starts, as <code>docker compose up</code> would. Name them only when a file holds services a universe should not run.</td></tr>
  <tr><td data-l="Field"><code>optional</code></td><td data-l="Meaning">Named groups started with <code>--with &lt;name&gt;</code>. The name is activated as a profile too, so a service can join a group by either route.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <div class="code"><pre><span class="k">apps</span>:
  - <span class="k">name</span>: api
    <span class="k">repo</span>: api
    <span class="k">services</span>: [web, db]
    <span class="k">optional</span>:
      - <span class="k">name</span>: workers
        <span class="k">services</span>: [worker, scheduler]</pre></div>
  <div class="code"><pre><span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl universe up 3 api --with workers</pre></div>
  <p><code>ls -o wide</code> shows every service with its state, including the ones that are off and the flag that would start them.</p>
</section>
</Page>
